@flexiarg institutions/nested-enterprises
@title Nested Enterprises
@keywords nesting, layers, scale, polycentricity, Ostrom 8, common-pool resources
@audience institution designers, commons researchers
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade principle
@provenance Ostrom design principle 8, wording as in Cox, Arnold and Tomas (2010), "A review of design principles for community-based natural resource management", Ecology and Society 15(4):38, quoted in Corneli 2016 (ICCC 2016) Table 1. Original source Ostrom 1990, Governing the Commons. Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29.
@status draft 2026-10-29; to vet against Ostrom 1990 and Cox et al. 2010 before relying on non-quoted text
@references [institutions/ARGUMENT creativity-commons/nested-places]

! conclusion:
  "Appropriation, provision, monitoring, enforcement, conflict resolution, and governance activities are organised in multiple layers of nested enterprises."

  + context:
    Applies to resources that are parts of larger systems (a local fishery within a regional stock).

  + IF:
    A resource is part of a larger system, or its users belong to larger groups.

  + HOWEVER:
    Layers multiply the places where decisions can stall or conflict.

  + THEN:
    Organise each governance activity at the scales it needs, with smaller units nested within larger ones.

  + BECAUSE:
    Problems arise at several scales at once; a single level of organisation is either too small for the large ones or too remote for the small ones.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Vet the HOWEVER and BECAUSE against Ostrom 1990 and Cox et al. 2010; only the conclusion is quoted.]
    next[Creativity-commons transposition: creativity-commons/nested-places.]
